418 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Memory updating is crucial for adaptability in both humans and animals, as it enables adjustments of behavior in a continuously changing world. Declarative memories allow us to recall facts and experiences, showcasing memory's flexibility, while threat learning results in implicit memories that prompt defensive behaviors. Despite their differences, both types of memory involve reactivation and change, helping us understand adaptation and the potential for maladaptive conditions like anxiety or PTSD.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
419 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Intriguingly, the mechanisms governing memory malleability are not yet fully described. Nowadays, it is acknowledged that external cues trigger memory reactivation. This reactivation has been proposed to mediate memory malleability, promoting the opening of a time window for changes in the original memory trace. Prediction error, the incongruence between what was learned and what occurs, plays a pivotal role in memory updating. We have demonstrated that the impact of a reminder cue, along with the characteristics of the original memory, can lead to selective changes such as strengthening, weakening, or updating of the memory. From here, we aim to explore the brain oscillation signatures that underpin cognitive and behavioral processes. In this presentation, I will outline our research journey, employing a behavioral approach, into two types of memories: declarative and aversive memories. Then, I will share experiments designed to investigate the neural activity elicited by reminders with prediction error, examining resting states before and after the reminder presentation. These experiments mark a significant step forward and raise new questions about the conditions that facilitate memory updating. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [11 12]
>>
endobj
