/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Introduction Congenital hydrocephalus is one of the most challenging paediatric diseases. Neurological impairment \(sensory, motor, memory and learning deficits\) of neonates with congenital hydrocephalus is not reversed by cerebrospinal fluid \(CSF\) derivative surgery. Goals Through the present investigation we have explored the ethiopathological process leading to hydrocephalus to explore new therapeutical approaches for children born with congenital hydrocephalus. Methods The brain of hydrocephalic hyh mice, HTx rats, and hydrocephalic human foetuses, were processed by optical and electron microscopy at different gestational stages, and they were further studied by using immunohistochemical procedures. Antibodies against Nestin \(neural stem cells\), GFAP \(astrocytes\), B3-tubulin \(neural progenitor\), and B4-tubulin \(ependyma\) were used as cell markers. The expression of cell junction proteins \(N-cadherin and connexins\) was also studied. Results and Discussion Abnormal expression of cell junction proteins in neural stem cells/ependyma appears as a final common pathway of the ethiopathological process leading to congenital hydrocephalus and outcomes: abnormal brain development and neural impairment. It stimulated us to explore a cell therapy based on the early grafting of neural stem cells into the CSF of hydrocephalic HTx rats. It resulted in the partial reparation of the germinal matrix and the improvement of specific neural functions, as assayed by using standardized neurological and cognitive tests \(NOR test\). These results open a new avenue for the treatment of congenital hydrocephalus as the culmination of a persistent research effort and the convergence of interdisciplinary capabilities at the University Austral de Chile and foreign associated laboratories. )
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [18 19 20 21]
>>
endobj
