/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (However, neonates do not showcase the same patterns of brain activity either before or during the administration of GABAergic drugs; for example, they not produce alpha oscillations. Instead, neonatal brains have been qualitatively characterized as having discontinuous \(e.g., non stationary\) EEG activity. Here we explored brain activity from 20 critically-ill term neonates \(37-44 weeks\) undergoing intensive care and requiring anesthesia for noncardiac surgery, and compared their EEG before and during a continuous rate propofol infusion. We evaluated changes in EEG activity due to propofol using two analysis strategies. First, we compared classical spectral measures averaged across time. Secondly, we segmented EEG data in time characterizing each 1s segment as isoelectric, lowvoltage, or high-voltage, based on peak amplitude. These EEG states were evaluated in terms of their occupancy, duration, number of distinct segments and internal spectral properties. We found that average spectral properties showed no significant difference due to propofol. Interestingly, neonates showed systematic increases in isoelectric EEG states occupancy, number of segments and duration. We also saw marked reductions of spectral power within low-voltage EEG states across all frequencies, but not within high-voltage states. These results introduce novel potential markers of hypnotic depth for neonates, and emphasize the usefulness of considering the dynamical and many times non-stationary nature of brain states. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [14 15]
>>
endobj
