/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Brain rhythms are fundamental to neural communication, enabling coordination across brain regions and supporting key cognitive functions such as perception, memory, and attention. In this talk, we explore how computational modeling, grounded in brain connectivity and EEG data, can be used to understand and modulate these rhythms through neurostimulation. We will present how large-scale brain network models allow us to simulate the effects of external rhythmic stimulation at different frequencies, revealing how specific neural circuits respond depending on their intrinsic dynamics and connectivity patterns. These simulations offer insight into the frequency-specific engagement of neural populations, and how external input can shift or enhance endogenous oscillatory activity. By bridging theory, simulation, and empirical EEG data, this approach provides a framework for designing targeted and personalized neuromodulation strategies. The implications extend to both basic neuroscience and clinical applications, offering potential for improving brain health, enhancing cognitive performance, and treating neurological disorders.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
