/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Spinal cord injury \(SCI\) causes permanent motor and sensory deficits due to the limited therapies and regenerative capacity of the mammalian central nervous system. In contrast, Xenopus laevis can regenerate its spinal cord during larval \(regenerative, R-stage\) stages, a capacity lost after metamorphosis \(non-regenerative, NRstage\). This makes Xenopus a powerful model for comparing regenerative and non-regenerative responses within the same species. Mitochondria are emerging as central regulators of neuronal survival, inflammation, axon regeneration, and neural stem progenitor cell \(NSPC\) behavior, processes critical to SCI. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
481 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (However, their temporal dynamics and metabolic adaptations during regeneration remain poorly understood. Here, we investigated these mitochondrial responses in R- and NR-stage Xenopus laevis following SCI, using electron and confocal microscopy, transgenic lines, RT-qPCR, enzymatic assays, and western blotting. In R-stage animals, SCI induced a rapid mitochondrial response in NSPCs, characterized by a reduction in mitochondrial number and an increase in swollen morphology without signs of dysfunction. This was accompanied by a shift toward aerobic glycolysis, supported by increased glycolytic transcript expression, enzymatic activity, and a transient decrease in mitochondrial membrane potential. In contrast, NR-stage animals showed a delayed mitochondrial response, mitochondrial dysfunction, and structural abnormalities. Despite this, ATP levels increased, and transcriptomic data revealed a shift toward fatty acid catabolism. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
