/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (consolidation of social fear memory via associative learning mechanisms, paralleling classical fear conditioning. We examine how the context of defeat becomes a conditioned stimulus, while the aggressor serves as the unconditioned stimulus, producing fear-related behaviors such as freezing. The consolidation and expression of social fear memory rely on distributed neural circuits that integrate threat appraisal, contextual cues, and social information. These include canonical fear-processing hubs, the amygdala, and prefrontal cortex, as well as regions specialized for social behavior such as the nucleus accumbens. Neuroendocrine systems including stress hormones, norepinephrine, oxytocin, and vasopressin modulate these pathways in context-dependent ways, allowing either social vigilance or buffering. Understanding social defeat as a form of fear memory highlights targets for intervention across acquisition to extinction phases. This framework provides a foundation for refining pharmacological and psychological treatments for stress-related disorders rooted in social trauma.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
