505 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In gregarious species, social behavior is one of the indispensable aspects of their lives. Therefore, it is not surprising that social variables influence neural, endocrine, and immune outcomes. One example is social buffering, the phenomenon in which the presence of affiliative conspecifics mitigates stress responses. We conducted a series of studies in rats to examine social buffering using auditory fear conditioning. When the fear-conditioned subjects were exposed to the conditioned stimulus \(CS\) alone, conditioned fear responses, including freezing, were observed. However, the presence of an unfamiliar same-sex rat \(associate\) completely blocked these responses, suggesting that social buffering ameliorates conditioned fear responses. Subsequent analyses revealed that the olfactory signal alone was necessary and sufficient for inducing social buffering. Parallel investigation of the characteristics of social buffering revealed that not all rats provided buffering effects to the Wistar subjects. Specifically, Wistar rats and the descendants of Wistar rats acted as social buffers, whereas rats established independently from Wistar rats were found to be non-buffers. Based on these findings, we searched for the chemicals responsible for social buffering within the body odor of the social buffers. We found that 2-methylbutyric acid alone was sufficient to induce social buffering. Therefore, we refer to it as an appeasing pheromone. We believe that a deeper understanding of social buffering could significantly advance our knowledge of sociality in animals, while also paving the way for novel approaches to fear regulation.)
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
